F. Investigation workspace — /investigations/[id]
Header:
- Title.
- Repository and pinned commit.
- Status.
- Created time.
- Retry retrieval when failed.
- Export report when evidence is ready.
Tabs:
Overview
- Original bug report.
- Expected/actual behavior.
- Submitted logs.
- Retrieval outcome.
- Explanation that retrieved files are candidates, not a verified root cause.
Evidence
- Ranked evidence list.
- File path and line range.
- Source type: code, test, or documentation.
- Matching snippet.
- Match reason: semantic similarity, exact symbol/text match, or both.
- Click an item to open the file viewer and highlight its lines.
- Link to GitHub at the pinned commit.
Do not present similarity scores as probabilities that a file caused the bug.
Activity
- Timestamped indexing/retrieval events.
- Real errors.
- Retry events.
Right-side panel:
- Current stage.
- Elapsed time.
- Retrieved chunk count.
- Index version.
Show Changes and Tests as disabled future tabs with clear “Available in a later milestone” explanations.
G. History — /investigations
- Search by title.
- Filter by repository and status.
- Sort newest first.
- Paginate.
- Open an investigation.
- Delete with confirmation.
H. Settings — /settings
- Theme preference.
- Backend/database/vector-store/worker health.
- Active embedding model and configured import limits.
- Explain that backend configuration comes from environment variables.
Theme is editable. Infrastructure configuration is read-only in P0.
6. Data models
Use UUID primary keys and UTC timestamps.
Model	Required fields
Repository	id, name, owner, github_url, branch, status, active_index_id, created_at, updated_at
RepositoryIndex	id, repository_id, commit_sha, status, embedding_model, embedding_dimension, file_count, chunk_count, skipped_count, error_message, started_at, completed_at
SourceFile	id, index_id, path, language, content_hash, content, line_count, indexing_status, skip_reason
CodeChunk	id, source_file_id, index_id, start_line, end_line, symbol_name, content, vector_point_id
Investigation	id, repository_id, index_id, title, description, expected_behavior, actual_behavior, logs, status, error_message, created_at, completed_at
Evidence	id, investigation_id, chunk_id, rank, semantic_score, exact_match, match_reason
ActivityEvent	id, repository_id nullable, investigation_id nullable, stage, level, message, created_at
Job	id, kind, repository_id nullable, investigation_id nullable, status, attempt, error_message, started_at, completed_at


Repository statuses: pending, indexing, ready, failed.
Investigation statuses: queued, retrieving, evidence_ready, failed.
Preserve old indexes while investigations reference them. Reindexing must not change historical evidence.
7. Indexing and retrieval behavior
Indexing
- Fetch into an application-managed directory.
- Resolve branch to an immutable commit.
- Parse Python using AST where possible.
- Prefer function/class chunks with surrounding context.
- Split large chunks into bounded overlapping sections.
- Fall back to line-based chunks on parsing errors.
- Split documentation by headings, then bounded line sections.
- Preserve accurate original line numbers.
- Store vectors with repository_id, index_id, file_path, and chunk_id metadata.
- Activate a new index only after indexing completes successfully.
- A failed reindex keeps the previous ready index usable.
Retrieval
Build the query from the description, expected/actual behavior, and logs.
1. Extract filenames and identifiers from the report.
2. Search exact matches within the pinned index.
3. Retrieve semantic candidates from Qdrant within that same index.
4. Combine rankings using reciprocal rank fusion.
5. Deduplicate chunks.
6. Return up to 12 evidence items, with a maximum of three per file.
7. Persist the evidence and ordering.
An empty result is a valid outcome: show “No relevant evidence found” and allow the user to refine the report.
P0 requires no generative LLM call. Its outputs must be backed by retrieved repository content.
8. API contract
All endpoints are under /api/v1.
Method	Endpoint	Behavior
GET	/health	Service health
GET	/dashboard	Counts and recent records
GET	/repositories	Searchable, paginated list
POST	/repositories	Validate, persist, queue import; return 202
GET	/repositories/{id}	Details and current indexing state
DELETE	/repositories/{id}	Queue cleanup; return 202
POST	/repositories/{id}/reindex	Queue new index; return 202
GET	/repositories/{id}/files	Files for a specified/current index
GET	/files/{id}	Stored content and metadata
GET	/investigations	Search/filter/pagination
POST	/investigations	Persist and queue retrieval; return 202
GET	/investigations/{id}	Inputs, status, evidence summary
GET	/investigations/{id}/evidence	Ranked evidence
GET	/investigations/{id}/events	Timestamped activity
POST	/investigations/{id}/retry	Retry failed retrieval
GET	/investigations/{id}/report	Download Markdown report
DELETE	/investigations/{id}	Delete investigation and related records


List responses use {items, total, page, page_size}.
Errors use {error: {code, message, field_errors?}}.
Return appropriate 404, 409, and 422 responses. Poll active jobs every two seconds; stop polling when terminal. Persist jobs and mark interrupted work retriable after worker restart.
9. Demo and setup
Include a small bundled Python repository containing:
- Application code.
- pytest tests.
- README.
- A documented seeded login bug.
Provide Load demo repository as an explicit action. It must run the same indexing and retrieval pipeline as imported repositories.
Do not silently populate fake investigations or fabricate successful results.
Deliver:
- .env.example without secrets.
- Database migrations.
- Docker Compose with persistent volumes.
- README with startup commands, first-run model download requirements, architecture, and troubleshooting.
- Clear indication when required services are unavailable.
10. Acceptance criteria
P0 is complete when:
1. The documented local startup process launches the frontend, backend, database, vector store, queue, and worker.
2. The dashboard starts with an accurate empty state.
3. A supported public repository can be imported and indexed at a stored commit SHA.
4. Invalid/private/unavailable repository URLs produce actionable errors.
5. Unsupported and oversized files are handled according to the stated limits.
6. Refreshing during indexing preserves status and progress.
7. A bug report produces persisted, repository-specific evidence.
8. Evidence paths, snippets, and line ranges match stored source content.
9. Evidence opens the correct file and immutable GitHub source link.
10. Retrieval never returns evidence from another repository or index.
11. Reindexing preserves historical investigations.
12. An investigation can be reopened after restarting services.
13. Report export contains the inputs, commit, evidence, and P0 limitations.
14. Failures show useful retry actions and never appear as success.
15. All primary controls work; future controls are visibly disabled.
16. Desktop/mobile layouts and keyboard navigation are usable.
17. Type checking, linting, backend tests, and the frontend production build pass.
Include focused tests for URL validation, chunk line accuracy, index isolation, failed reindex behavior, and persisted investigation retrieval. Include one end-to-end demo flow.
11. Implementation order
1. Scaffold services, environment configuration, and database migrations.
2. Build navigation, shared components, and empty states.
3. Implement repository import and persistent indexing jobs.
4. Implement source browsing and filtered retrieval.
5. Implement investigation creation and evidence workspace.
6. Add history, report export, settings, and demo loading.
7. Verify acceptance criteria and document remaining limitations.
Build P0 end to end. Mark it complete only after repository indexing and evidence retrieval work against real source files.